{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And the serpent said, etc.</b> By doubling to say <b>you shall not surely die</b>, he intended to remove two kinds of death that she alluded to in her words — whether on the grounds that the tree itself kills and it appears to be a deadly poison, or on the grounds that she feared lest YHWH strike them with plague. He said: ״לֹא מוֹת תְּמֻתוּן״ — not a natural death, not a punitive death. And from this the difficulty now arises: if so, why would YHWH command not to eat, as I explained in connection with Eve's words. Therefore he came and gave a new reason, and set his mouth in the heavens, for the reason that kept them from it is lest they become like Him, for ״בְּיוֹם אֲכָלְכֶם״ etc. — they would become like Elohim. Now the wicked one found an opening to say these things, since having preceded the creation of man and Eve, he found an opening to say that he knows something that those who came after him did not know. Also, since he was barred from entering the Garden of Eden, he found an opening to say that permission to enter the Garden was granted only to them who do not know the secret of the matter, and the tree was forbidden to them — but not so for him who knows the secret of the matter; YHWH did not permit him to enter there. And furthermore, according to those words, once they eat there is no fear of Elohim, for they will be like Him and He will have no dominion to put them to death — and in this he drove in the nail of idolatry. And is it not for you to know that concerning idolatry one is judged even on the thought (Kiddushin 40a)." "<b>Further,</b> it intends by saying <b>you shall not surely die</b>, according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 19:3), that he pushed her and demonstrated that she did not die upon touching; therefore the wicked one doubled to say: just as she did not die from touching, as she herself said, so too she would not die from eating. Now this proof exists for one of the two reasons that the woman gave, as I explained above — namely the concern that the tree of itself kills: for this distinction there is proof, for if the tree kills on touch, behold she touched it and did not die. But regarding what she also intended to say — that she feared Elohim who commanded her lest He strike — there is no proof from the fact that she did not die on touch, for the punishment He decreed upon her still stands, and we return to the reason we explained above." "<b>And if</b> you say: ״Why would YHWH create such an evil creature as a stumbling block?״ Know that regarding this creature, the more YHWH marvelled in the greatness of its dimension, the greater is the dimension of the soul's attainment in the supernal world, for according to the intensity of effort required for a person to achieve victory, so too does he receive his reward, as they said of blessed memory (Avot 5:23): ״לְפוּם צַעֲרָא אַגְרָא״ (according to the struggle, so the reward). And if He had set the measure of the tempter's power at a small measure, there would be no room for one who wishes to attain a great attainment to achieve it, for there is no attainment without the intermediary of vanquishing the tempter. Go forth and learn from what is said in the holy Zohar (Terumah 166b) by way of the parable of a king's son whom his father commanded not to approach a harlot, and the harlot He commanded to tempt him, to test whether the son honors his father — see there. And from this YHWH marvelled to perform kindness for His beloved, to prepare for them something through whose intermediacy they may ascend from degree to degree of eternal life — happy is the people that has it so."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:38:52.848337792Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}